WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.499
Part one ended with Jordan blocks.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:07.639
Now let's put them to work.

3
00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.391
Take the two by two block with zero

4
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:12.531
point nine on the diagonal

5
00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:14.060
and a one above it.

6
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.233
Both eigenvalues are zero point nine

7
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:18.606
smaller than one

8
00:00:18.606 --> 00:00:21.810
so the powers of J go to zero in the end.

9
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.295
But do they shrink from the very first step?

10
00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:28.080
Pause and predict.

11
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.183
Write J as lambda I plus N

12
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:38.108
where N holds the one above the diagonal.

13
00:00:38.108 --> 00:00:39.883
N squared is zero

14
00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:43.060
and N commutes with the identity.

15
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.207
So the binomial theorem works

16
00:00:46.207 --> 00:00:48.072
and it stops early.

17
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:51.802
J to the k is lambda to the k, times I

18
00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:54.319
plus k times lambda to the

19
00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:56.930
power k minus one, times N.

20
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.713
On the diagonal sits lambda to the k.

21
00:01:00.806 --> 00:01:03.868
In the corner sits k times lambda

22
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.280
to the power k minus one.

23
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.929
With lambda equal to zero point nine

24
00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:12.086
the corner starts at one

25
00:01:12.086 --> 00:01:13.911
then one point eight

26
00:01:13.911 --> 00:01:16.150
then two point four three.

27
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:16.980
It grows.

28
00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.147
It peaks at about three point eight seven

29
00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:24.509
at k equal to nine and again at ten.

30
00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:28.235
Only then does the shrinking factor win

31
00:01:28.235 --> 00:01:31.960
one point four one at k equal to thirty

32
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.050
and zero point two nine at fifty.

33
00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.990
So beware the rule

34
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.925
if every eigenvalue is smaller than one

35
00:01:39.925 --> 00:01:42.358
the powers shrink from the start.

36
00:01:42.358 --> 00:01:43.790
Where does it hold?

37
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.580
The rule holds for zero point

38
00:01:46.656 --> 00:01:48.484
nine times the identity

39
00:01:48.484 --> 00:01:50.922
which has the same eigenvalues.

40
00:01:50.922 --> 00:01:53.818
Its size is zero point nine to the k

41
00:01:53.818 --> 00:01:55.570
smaller at every step.

42
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.729
The Jordan block, with the same eigenvalues

43
00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:02.240
first climbs to almost four.

44
00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.632
The eigenvalues decide where

45
00:02:04.705 --> 00:02:06.018
the powers end up.

46
00:02:06.018 --> 00:02:08.790
The Jordan blocks decide the journey.

47
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.221
Exponentials work the same way.

48
00:02:12.221 --> 00:02:15.440
E to the J t is e to the lambda t

49
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.280
times e to the N t

50
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.866
and that series stops after two terms

51
00:02:20.866 --> 00:02:21.970
I plus t N.

52
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.730
So e to the J t has e to the

53
00:02:24.816 --> 00:02:26.967
lambda t on the diagonal

54
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:30.840
and t times e to the lambda t in the corner.

55
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.229
For the barrels, this gives x two equal to

56
00:02:35.326 --> 00:02:37.634
four t e to the minus t.

57
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.289
The mysterious factor t from part one

58
00:02:41.385 --> 00:02:44.270
is the corner of e to the N t.

59
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.927
A block of size m brings t

60
00:02:47.016 --> 00:02:49.234
to the power m minus one.

61
00:02:49.234 --> 00:02:53.050
Three barrels in a cascade give t squared

62
00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.180
times e to the minus t.

63
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.374
Now change the barrels slightly

64
00:02:58.456 --> 00:03:00.334
let barrel two drain at

65
00:03:00.416 --> 00:03:02.213
rate b instead of one.

66
00:03:02.213 --> 00:03:04.174
For b not equal to one

67
00:03:04.255 --> 00:03:06.624
the eigenvalues minus one and

68
00:03:06.706 --> 00:03:08.421
minus b are distinct

69
00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:11.280
so the matrix can be diagonalized.

70
00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.408
But as b approaches one

71
00:03:13.408 --> 00:03:16.222
the two eigenvectors become parallel.

72
00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:19.185
The condition number of the eigenvector

73
00:03:19.259 --> 00:03:21.925
matrix is about twenty, two hundred

74
00:03:21.925 --> 00:03:23.259
and two thousand

75
00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:25.481
for b equal to one point one

76
00:03:25.481 --> 00:03:26.962
one point zero one

77
00:03:26.962 --> 00:03:29.110
and one point zero zero one.

78
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.284
Splitting the start into eigenvectors needs

79
00:03:33.373 --> 00:03:36.664
huge coefficients that nearly cancel

80
00:03:36.664 --> 00:03:39.865
plus and minus forty, four hundred

81
00:03:39.865 --> 00:03:41.199
four thousand.

82
00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:44.401
Yet the solution itself stays calm

83
00:03:44.401 --> 00:03:47.780
and tends to four t e to the minus t.

84
00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.473
So the Jordan form jumps

85
00:03:50.473 --> 00:03:54.128
diagonal for every b not equal to one

86
00:03:54.128 --> 00:03:57.690
and a single block at b equal to one.

87
00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.333
It gets worse

88
00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:01.915
the eigenvalues of a Jordan block

89
00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:03.814
are extremely sensitive.

90
00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.320
Put a tiny epsilon in the bottom

91
00:04:06.396 --> 00:04:08.750
left corner of the n by n block

92
00:04:08.826 --> 00:04:10.877
with zeros on the diagonal.

93
00:04:10.953 --> 00:04:13.003
The eigenvalues jump to the

94
00:04:13.079 --> 00:04:14.750
n-th roots of epsilon.

95
00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.625
With n equal to ten and epsilon

96
00:04:17.706 --> 00:04:20.149
equal to ten to the minus ten

97
00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:22.837
they move out to zero point one.

98
00:04:22.918 --> 00:04:26.257
A change in the tenth decimal place moves

99
00:04:26.338 --> 00:04:28.700
the eigenvalues in the first.

100
00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.131
Rounding errors are enough.

101
00:04:31.208 --> 00:04:33.366
For the exact integer matrix

102
00:04:33.443 --> 00:04:34.676
G from part one

103
00:04:34.676 --> 00:04:37.991
NumPy returns three different eigenvalues

104
00:04:38.068 --> 00:04:40.149
each about ten to the minus

105
00:04:40.226 --> 00:04:41.690
five away from two.

106
00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.669
So no numerical library computes

107
00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:45.901
a Jordan form.

108
00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:49.104
It would have to decide that different

109
00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:51.240
numbers are really equal.

110
00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.259
What computers use instead

111
00:04:54.353 --> 00:04:56.041
is the Schur form.

112
00:04:56.041 --> 00:04:58.480
A equals Q T Q transpose

113
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.420
with Q orthogonal and T upper triangular.

114
00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:06.186
The eigenvalues sit on the diagonal of T.

115
00:05:06.186 --> 00:05:09.268
This form asks for less than Jordan

116
00:05:09.268 --> 00:05:12.433
triangular instead of block diagonal

117
00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:14.599
and that makes it robust

118
00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:16.932
Q has condition number one

119
00:05:17.015 --> 00:05:19.681
and the Q R algorithm of Lecture

120
00:05:19.764 --> 00:05:21.930
thirty computes it stably.

121
00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:26.574
For the perturbed barrels, T is minus b

122
00:05:26.574 --> 00:05:28.936
one, zero, minus one.

123
00:05:28.936 --> 00:05:31.727
It moves smoothly with b

124
00:05:31.727 --> 00:05:34.840
while the Jordan form jumps.

125
00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.449
The SVD is even calmer.

126
00:05:37.547 --> 00:05:41.672
A perturbation of size epsilon moves every

127
00:05:41.771 --> 00:05:45.110
singular value by at most epsilon.

128
00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.110
A Jordan block is lambda I plus N

129
00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.601
which explains the corner terms in

130
00:05:50.672 --> 00:05:52.665
its powers and exponentials.

131
00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:55.156
Powers can grow before they decay.

132
00:05:55.227 --> 00:05:57.149
And because the Jordan form

133
00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.999
jumps under tiny changes

134
00:05:58.999 --> 00:06:01.490
we compute with Schur and the SVD.

135
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:02.586
Try this

136
00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:05.900
put ten to the minus nine in the corner

137
00:06:05.983 --> 00:06:08.137
of a three by three block.

138
00:06:08.137 --> 00:06:10.872
How far do its eigenvalues move?

139
00:06:10.872 --> 00:06:13.440
And that brings us to the end.
